course: dictionary
lesson: 11755
title: Bankrupt: when money runs out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Bankrupt means you cannot pay back the money you owe. «The shop owed more money than it could ever pay back, so it went bankrupt.» The word owe is the clue: the shop had debts it could not pay.
explain: A court often steps in when someone is bankrupt. It takes what they own and gives it to the people they owe. «The court sold the bakery's ovens and tables to pay the bakers and the farmers.» Here the words sold and pay show what bankrupt leads to.
explain: Bankrupt can also be used in a playful way. «The tired writer was bankrupt of ideas.» Nobody is talking about money here. The clue is of ideas: she had none left, so she could not write any more.
fact: Bankrupt means unable to pay your debts.
fact: A business can go bankrupt when it owes more money than it can ever pay back.
fact: «Bankrupt» can also be a verb: «The bad debts bankrupted the family firm» means the debts made the firm bankrupt.
fact: Informal use: «bankrupt of» something means having none of it left, as in «bankrupt of ideas».
fact: In the game Monopoly, a player who cannot pay is bankrupt and out of the game.
exercise: «The shop owed more money than it could ever pay back.» What does bankrupt mean? | Has lots of money in the bank | *Cannot pay its debts | Has just opened for business | Sells only toys
exercise: «The bad loans bankrupted the family firm.» What does bankrupted mean here? | Moved it to a new town | Thanked it for its work | *Made it bankrupt | Painted it bright colours
exercise: «The writer was bankrupt of ideas.» What does bankrupt of ideas mean? | *Had none left | Had more ideas than anyone else | Wrote a very long book | Was selling ideas in a shop
test: «Toad spent so wildly on cars that his friends feared for his money.» What does this suggest about Toad? | He saves every penny he earns | He has no cars at all | *He spends more money than he has | He is the richest animal in the wood
test: Which word means a person who cannot pay their debts? | curious | generous | *bankrupt | jolly
exam: Which sentence uses bankrupt as a verb? | *The bad debts bankrupted the bakery. | The bakery was bankrupt and sad. | Bankrupt is a lovely bakery name. | The bakery went bankrupt last year.
